<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  warehouse.sdf . the M5 autonomy world for the amr agent project.

  THE OWNER RULED ON 2026 07 30 THAT M5 SLAM AND Nav2 RUN HERE, not in the
  M4 commissioning arena: autonomy needs aisles and racks to be meaningful,
  and M6 enlarges THIS world to ten stations, so the map artifact and the
  Nav2 tuning carry forward instead of being discarded. The arena
  (worlds/forklift_arena.sdf) keeps its M4 commissioning role, and neither
  world includes the other.

  No vehicle is embedded here. agv/forklift/model.sdf is a plain <model> and
  is spawned by sim/launch/warehouse_bringup.launch.py, so the world and the
  vehicle stay separately owned.

  NO CONTROL LOGIC LIVES HERE, and nothing here is a safety device. Every
  model below is geometry: it stands still and the vehicle scanners measure
  whatever range it presents. Deciding what a range means belongs to the PLC
  standard program and to the vehicle nodes (invariants 5, 6 and 9). The
  protective stop, the e stop chain and safe torque off are onboard and
  hardwired and appear in no world file (invariant 1).

  ==================== THE HONESTY RULE THIS WORLD OBEYS ====================

  A long featureless aisle is a DEGENERATE DIRECTION for scan matching, and
  no slam_toolbox parameter fixes it. So the structure below exists for one
  reason only: a real warehouse has it. Rack uprights, rack ends, stock that
  is present in some bays and absent in others, a central cross aisle, two
  end aisles, building columns, a dock door frame, a transfer station frame
  and a charging area. NOTHING was scattered into an aisle because scan
  matching struggles without it.

  Where the honest world still leaves a degenerate stretch, that stretch is
  MEASURED AND NAMED in worlds/WAREHOUSE_LANDMARKS.md rather than landscaped
  away. Read that file before tuning any localisation parameter against this
  world: it predicts, from geometry alone and before any SLAM run, where
  localisation is strong and where it is weak.

  ==================== LAYOUT (top view, metres) ====================

  30.0 x 20.0 clear floor, origin at the centre of the hall, inner wall
  faces at x = +-15.00 and y = +-10.00. Walls 0.20 thick and 4.00 high.

     +y
      ^   north wall, inner face y = +10.00
      |  ####################  RackRowA  (single deep, against the wall)
      |                                          y in [8.90, 10.00]
      |  ======== AISLE A, centreline y = +7.00, 3.80 m clear ========
      |  ####################  RackRowB  (back to back pair)
      |                                          y in [2.60, 5.10]
      |  ======== AISLE B, centreline y = +0.65, 3.90 m clear ========
      |  ####################  RackRowC  (back to back pair)
      |                                          y in [-3.80, -1.30]
      |
      |  ==== DOCK APRON, y in [-10.00, -3.80], 6.20 m deep ====
      |  [ChargeBay1] [ChargeBay2]        [DoorGap x in [4.00, 8.00]]
      +======================================================> +x
         west end aisle          central cross aisle       east end aisle
         x in [-15.00, -11.00]   x in [-1.80, +1.80]       x in [11.00, 15.00]

  Each rack row is cut into a WEST RUN (x in [-11.00, -1.80]) and an EAST
  RUN (x in [+1.80, +11.00]) by the central cross aisle, and both runs stop
  4.00 m short of the side walls, leaving an end aisle at each end. That is
  the ordinary way a rack block is set out, and it is also what puts a rack
  END, rather than an unbroken wall, at every aisle mouth.

  ==================== RACK GEOMETRY, BY RULE ====================

  Every rack primitive follows from these numbers, so any one of them can be
  checked by arithmetic rather than by trusting the file:

    upright section        0.10 (x) by 0.08 (y), 4.00 high
    bay pitch              2.30 m, so a 9.20 m run is 5 frames and 4 bays
    frame x, west run      -11.00, -8.70, -6.40, -4.10, -1.80
    frame x, east run       +1.80, +4.10, +6.40, +8.70, +11.00
    bay centre x           midway between adjacent frames
    sub row depth          1.10 m, uprights 0.04 m inside each face
    back to back flue      0.30 m between the two sub rows of a pair
    stock unit             2.10 (x) by 1.00 (y), set 0.05 m back from the
                           aisle face, so the uprights stand proud of it
    floor level stock      z in [0.00, 1.40], present in EVERY bay
    reserve level stock    z in [1.50, 2.70], present per the rule below

  RESERVE LEVEL OCCUPANCY IS THE ONE THING THE SCAN PLANE ACTUALLY SEES, so
  it is stated as a stock state rather than chosen ray by ray:

    east runs   all four bays loaded. Goods in arrives at the dock door,
                which is on the east half of the south wall, and is put away
                nearest the door first.
    west runs   bays 0 and 2 loaded, bays 1 and 3 empty. The west runs are
                the picked face and are partly depleted.

  This is a warehouse fact with a consequence, not a tuning knob. It makes
  the east half of each aisle a pair of nearly flat parallel faces and the
  west half a broken one, and WAREHOUSE_LANDMARKS.md measures exactly what
  that costs. The pattern was NOT chosen to make scan matching work; it was
  chosen, stated, and then measured.

  ==================== WHAT THE SCAN PLANES SEE ====================

  The vehicle carries three planar scanners (agv/forklift/model.sdf):

    navigation lidar   z = 1.80 m, 360 samples over 360 deg, 0.10 to 8.00 m
    safety scanners    z = 0.15 m, 275 samples over 275 deg, 0.10 to 5.50 m
                       (a NON SAFE measurement channel; see agv/forklift/README.md)

  At z = 1.80 (navigation)   perimeter walls, rack uprights, RESERVE level
                             stock, building columns, dock door posts,
                             transfer station frame posts, both charger
                             cabinets.
  At z = 0.15 (safety)       perimeter walls, rack uprights, FLOOR level
                             stock, building columns, dock door posts,
                             transfer station frame posts, both charger
                             cabinets, the ConveyorStation deck.
  Seen by neither            the painted floor markings (0.006 thick), the
                             dock door lintel (z = 3.50 to 3.80).

  RACK BEAMS ARE NOT MODELLED. Horizontal beams sit wholly below (1.45) and
  above (2.75) the navigation plane and are never contacted by anything in
  this project, so modelling them would add of the order of 160 visual
  primitives to a software rasterised scene without changing one measured
  number. The uprights carry the collision, which is what a vehicle at floor
  level can actually hit.

  ==================== CHARGING BAYS ====================

  Two charging bays, added on the owner's instruction of 2026 07 31. The
  world owes GEOMETRY PLUS NAMES and nothing else: there is no docking
  behaviour, no approach logic, no charging state and no PLC or fleet
  interaction anywhere in this file. The planning that uses these bays comes
  with fleet management at M6, and it will cite worlds/WAREHOUSE_EVIDENCE.md
  section 5 for the poses rather than re measuring this file.

    ChargeBay1Marking   painted outline, 1.80 (x) by 3.20 (y), centre
                        (-9.80, -7.70)
    ChargeBay1Cabinet   red cabinet, 0.90 by 0.65 by 2.00, centre
                        (-9.80, -9.675), back against the south wall
    ChargeBay2Marking   painted outline, 1.80 (x) by 3.20 (y), centre
                        (-7.40, -7.70)
    ChargeBay2Cabinet   red cabinet, 0.90 by 0.65 by 2.00, centre
                        (-7.40, -9.675), back against the south wall

  The marking is sized from the vehicle's REAL PLAN ENVELOPE and not from
  its chassis box: agv/forklift/model.sdf puts the tines at x = -1.875 and
  the counterweight at x = +0.860, so the vehicle is 2.735 m long, and the
  two safety scanner brackets put it at 1.040 m wide. A 3.20 by 1.80 bay
  holds that with 0.23 m at each end and 0.38 m at each side. The 1.40 by
  0.90 chassis box would have been the wrong dimension by 1.335 m.

  THE MARKING IS PAINT. It has no collision, it is 6 mm proud of the floor,
  and neither scan plane sees it, exactly as the arena's markings behave.
  The CABINET is not paint: it is a physical box that the forklift can see
  and would collide with. Being 2.00 m tall it crosses both scan planes and
  presents the same 0.90 by 0.65 footprint to each.

  THE M3 ERA `ChargerStation` BLOCK IS GONE, NOT DUPLICATED. That model was
  a 0.80 by 0.80 by 1.20 placeholder against the west wall, written before
  any vehicle existed, and it topped out 0.60 m BELOW the navigation scan
  plane so the vehicle could never have seen it. Leaving it beside two
  bays that are also called charging would have left two different things
  called a charger in one world. The two bays replace it.

  ==================== SAFETY ZONE MARKING ====================

  SafetyZoneMarking repeats the arena's pattern (forklift_arena.sdf) so the
  M5 safety demonstration and the M5 autonomy demonstration can be recorded
  in ONE world: a 3.00 by 3.00 painted outline on open floor, four visual
  only strips, no collision, invisible to both scan planes. It sits where
  the central cross aisle meets the dock apron, which is the crossing a
  monitored zone would actually watch.

  THIS IS PAINT, NOT A SENSOR. Nothing in this file watches whether the
  vehicle is inside the outline and no crossing is detected here. The zone
  F input it stands for is an engineering stand in, latched by the F CPU
  safety program from a value the owner writes by hand at the moment a
  viewer sees the model cross the line (plc/forklift-safety/SPEC.md
  section 7). That program implements the SF 07 pattern, never SF 07
  itself.

  BOTH THE CHARGE BAY OUTLINES AND THE SAFETY ZONE OUTLINE ARE RED, on the
  owner's instruction that the bays use the same visual idiom. They are told
  apart by size and place, not by colour: the safety zone is 3.00 square on
  the cross aisle, a charge bay is 1.80 by 3.20 against the south wall.

  ==================== SENSOR SYSTEM AND RENDER COST ====================

  All three vehicle scanners are gz "gpu_lidar" sensors, so this world MUST
  load gz-sim-sensors-system with a render engine. gz's stock empty.sdf does
  not, and on it the vehicle drives while every scan topic stays silent.

  Rendering on the target machine is llvmpipe software rasterisation
  (sim/setup/WSL_ENVIRONMENT.md), so the scene is deliberately cheap: box
  primitives only, no mesh, no texture, no shadow, one directional light.
  This world carries substantially more primitives than the arena because a
  rack is made of uprights, and NO REAL TIME FACTOR FIGURE FOR IT EXISTS
  YET: the run that produced worlds/WAREHOUSE_EVIDENCE.md shared the machine
  with another agent's simulator, which makes any timing number worthless
  (docs/LESSONS.md 2026 07 30). That measurement is owed and is named in the
  report.

  The physics step is the arena's 2 ms at 500 Hz and not the 20 ms this file
  carried in its m3 form, so the M4 steer, traction and fork tuning figures
  measured in the arena remain comparable here.
-->
<sdf version="1.8">
  <world name="warehouse">

    <!-- The arena's 2 ms fixed step at 500 Hz, so joint rates measured on
         the M4 arena stay comparable here. -->
    <physics type="ode">
      <max_step_size>0.002</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
      <real_time_update_rate>500</real_time_update_rate>
    </physics>

    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::Physics" filename="libgz-sim-physics-system.so"/>
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands" filename="libgz-sim-user-commands-system.so"/>
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster" filename="libgz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system.so"/>

    <!-- Required by the vehicle's three gpu_lidar sensors. Without this no
         scan topic is ever advertised and the failure is silent. -->
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors" filename="libgz-sim-sensors-system.so">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>

    <!-- ======================= GUI CONFIGURATION ====================== -->
    <!--
      THIS BLOCK IS WHY THE LIDAR BEAMS ARE VISIBLE, and it is the arena's
      block with the camera moved. worlds/forklift_arena.sdf carries the
      full measured rationale for every plugin here and is the file to read
      and to edit; it is not repeated line for line because two copies of a
      long explanation drift apart. The three facts a viewer of THIS world
      needs are repeated, because getting them wrong is silent:

        1. `<visualize>true</visualize>` on a sensor is NECESSARY BUT NOT
           SUFFICIENT on Gazebo Harmonic. VisualizeLidar is what draws laser
           scans, it is not in gz sim's default GUI configuration, and it
           never reads the sensor's visualize flag. Loading it is this
           block's whole job.
        2. Nothing is drawn until a human presses the plugin's refresh
           button. Beams on is not the start state of this world.
        3. Refresh selects entry ZERO of a sorted topic list, which is
           /forklift/gz/safety_scanner_front/measurement, NOT the navigation
           lidar. Those beams are a non safe measurement channel and are not
           a protective field (invariant 1). A recording that leaves the
           default selection is showing the wrong sensor with the most
           dangerous available caption. The navigation lidar is
           /forklift/gz/scan_nav.

      The scene settings below are repeated from the world's <scene> element
      on purpose: MinimalScene does not inherit them.
    -->
    <gui fullscreen="0">

      <plugin filename="MinimalScene" name="3D View">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>3D View</title>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">docked</property>
        </gz-gui>
        <engine>ogre2</engine>
        <scene>scene</scene>
        <ambient_light>1.0 1.0 1.0</ambient_light>
        <background_color>0.7 0.7 0.7</background_color>
        <!-- Looking north east from over the south west corner, so the
             dock apron, both charging bays and the west end of the rack
             block are in frame with no camera move. -->
        <camera_pose>-19.0 -16.0 12.0 0 0.52 0.72</camera_pose>
      </plugin>

      <plugin filename="GzSceneManager" name="Scene Manager">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <plugin filename="InteractiveViewControl" name="Interactive view control">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <plugin filename="CameraTracking" name="Camera Tracking">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <plugin filename="EntityContextMenuPlugin" name="Entity context menu">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- NOT start_paused. The scanners are always_on but the sensors
           system renders on simulation steps, so a paused world publishes
           no scan, the plugin's topic list comes up empty and the beams
           never appear. The world is launched with -r for the same
           reason. -->
      <plugin filename="WorldControl" name="World control">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>World control</title>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="bool" key="resizable">false</property>
          <property type="double" key="height">72</property>
          <property type="double" key="z">1</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">floating</property>
          <anchors target="3D View">
            <line own="left" target="left"/>
            <line own="bottom" target="bottom"/>
          </anchors>
        </gz-gui>
        <play_pause>true</play_pause>
        <step>true</step>
        <start_paused>false</start_paused>
        <use_event>true</use_event>
      </plugin>

      <!-- The real time factor readout, on screen for any recording. It is
           NOT a measurement instrument: the value it shows is gz's smoothed
           instantaneous factor. -->
      <plugin filename="WorldStats" name="World stats">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>World stats</title>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="bool" key="resizable">false</property>
          <property type="double" key="height">110</property>
          <property type="double" key="width">290</property>
          <property type="double" key="z">1</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">floating</property>
          <anchors target="3D View">
            <line own="right" target="right"/>
            <line own="bottom" target="bottom"/>
          </anchors>
        </gz-gui>
        <sim_time>true</sim_time>
        <real_time>true</real_time>
        <real_time_factor>true</real_time_factor>
        <iterations>true</iterations>
      </plugin>

      <!-- The beams. Docked rather than floating so the topic and display
           type combo boxes are visible without hunting: which scanner is on
           screen is not guessable from the picture. -->
      <plugin filename="VisualizeLidar" name="Visualize Lidar">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>Visualize lidar</title>
          <property type="string" key="state">docked</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <plugin filename="Screenshot" name="Screenshot">
        <gz-gui>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">floating</property>
          <property type="double" key="width">100</property>
          <property type="double" key="height">50</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

    </gui>

    <scene>
      <ambient>1 1 1 1</ambient>
      <background>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</background>
      <shadows>false</shadows>
      <grid>false</grid>
    </scene>

    <light name="sun" type="directional">
      <cast_shadows>false</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 12 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>1.0 1.0 1.0 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
      <direction>-0.3 0.3 -1.0</direction>
    </light>

    <gravity>0 0 -9.8</gravity>

    <!-- ============================ Floor ============================ -->
    <!-- Plain concrete, mu 1.0 as in the arena: the tricycle has a single
         driven wheel and a slippery floor turns traction figures into tyre
         slip figures. -->
    <model name="Floor">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry>
            <plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>30 20</size></plane>
          </geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>1.0</mu2></ode></friction>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry>
            <plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>30 20</size></plane>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.55 0.55 0.55 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.55 0.55 0.55 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ======================= Perimeter walls ======================= -->
    <!-- 0.20 thick, 4.00 high, inner faces at x = +-15.00 and
         y = +-10.00. The south wall is in two segments leaving the
         4.00 m dock door opening at x in [4.00, 8.00]. -->
    <model name="WallNorth">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="wallnorth_collision">
          <pose>0.000 10.100 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>30.600 0.200 4.000</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="wallnorth_visual">
          <pose>0.000 10.100 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>30.600 0.200 4.000</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="WallSouthWest">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="wallsouthwest_collision">
          <pose>-5.600 -10.100 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>19.200 0.200 4.000</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="wallsouthwest_visual">
          <pose>-5.600 -10.100 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>19.200 0.200 4.000</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="WallSouthEast">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="wallsoutheast_collision">
          <pose>11.600 -10.100 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>7.200 0.200 4.000</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="wallsoutheast_visual">
          <pose>11.600 -10.100 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>7.200 0.200 4.000</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="WallEast">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="walleast_collision">
          <pose>15.100 0.000 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.200 20.000 4.000</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="walleast_visual">
          <pose>15.100 0.000 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.200 20.000 4.000</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="WallWest">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="wallwest_collision">
          <pose>-15.100 0.000 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.200 20.000 4.000</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="wallwest_visual">
          <pose>-15.100 0.000 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.200 20.000 4.000</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.80 0.75 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ========================= Dock door frame ====================== -->
    <!-- Two posts and a lintel marking the 4.00 m opening in the south
         wall. The opening itself stays free so the vehicle can drive
         through; the PLC controlled door acts here at M6 (ADR 0010). The
         posts cross both scan planes, the lintel crosses neither. -->
    <model name="DoorGap">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>6.00 -10.10 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="post_west_collision">
          <pose>-2.100 0.000 1.750 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.300 3.500</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="post_west_visual">
          <pose>-2.100 0.000 1.750 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.300 3.500</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.90 0.60 0.10 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.60 0.10 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="post_east_collision">
          <pose>2.100 0.000 1.750 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.300 3.500</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="post_east_visual">
          <pose>2.100 0.000 1.750 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.300 3.500</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.90 0.60 0.10 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.60 0.10 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="lintel_collision">
          <pose>0.000 0.000 3.650 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>4.400 0.300 0.300</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="lintel_visual">
          <pose>0.000 0.000 3.650 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>4.400 0.300 0.300</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.90 0.60 0.10 1</ambient><diffuse>0.90 0.60 0.10 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ============================ Racking =========================== -->
    <!-- Six models: three rows, each cut into a west and an east run by the
         central cross aisle. Every primitive follows the parametric rules
         in the file header, so any upright or stock unit can be checked by
         arithmetic. Row A is single deep against the north wall; rows B and
         C are back to back pairs with a 0.30 m flue. -->

    <model name="RackRowA_West">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <!-- sub row A, front face y = +8.90, rear face y = +10.00 -->
        <collision name="a_frame0_front_collision">
          <pose>-11.000 8.940 2.000 0 0 0</pose>
